nderline; color: #006699;" rel=3D"noopener"></a>to receive benefits. This w=
ould be for a minority of claimants =E2=80=94 about 10 per cent =E2=80=94 a=
nd there would be no phone, video or face-to-face assessments involved.</p>
<p style=3D"margin:0;font-size:18px;mso-line-height-alt:21px">&nbsp;</p><p =
style=3D"margin:0;font-size:18px;mso-line-height-alt:27px">Other claimants =
will face a new =E2=80=9Cimpact=E2=80=9D assessment process, which looks at=
 their actual costs. Benefit payments could then be tailored to meet their =
needs rather than being paid at the standard rate of =C2=A3107 a week, or t=
he enhanced rate of =C2=A3194 a week.</p><p style=3D"margin:0;font-size:18p=
x;mso-line-height-alt:21px">&nbsp;</p><p=20
style=3D"margin:0;font-size:18px;mso-line-height-alt:27px">
<strong>What about Pip?</strong> The decision to keep personal independence=
 payments (Pip) for claimants over 25 will lead to questions about whether =
the government will deliver on its pledge to reduce the benefits bill. The =
Institute for Fiscal Studies, a think tank, has warned that making it easie=
r for some people to claim disability benefits could&nbsp;<a href=3D"https:=
